\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Bulb dimming (4\_ch02 MC Q22).} A bulb is connected to an ideal battery. Which makes it dimmest: a low or high resistance in series, or either resistance in parallel?

A series resistor raises total resistance and reduces current. For the bulb, $P_b=I^2R_b$, so a high series resistance gives the smallest $I$ and the dimmest bulb. An added parallel branch does not change the bulb's p.d. when the battery is ideal, so its power is unchanged.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{What happens when a bulb burns out?} One bulb X is in series with a parallel pair Y and Z. All bulbs are identical. Z burns out. Predict X and Y.

Initially the load is $R+R/2=3R/2$. After Z opens, the load is $R+R=2R$, so total current decreases: X becomes dimmer. The parallel section changes from $R/2$ to $R$ and receives a larger fraction of the supply voltage; Y becomes brighter. The battery current decreases.
\end{exercise}

